  "description": "A parametric RENDER LOOK on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON THE CANVAS. It is the only canvas-hosted object here, and deliberately: engine, world, sun and exposure belong to the sheet, not to any element on it. It pairs with `camera`, which is where the shot lives.\n\nIT HAS NO 3D. A scene is not a thing in the scene. The card in plan is something to select and nothing more.\n\nTHE SUN IS THE ONE LIGHT MEASURED IN IRRADIANCE. Every fitting in this library converts lumens to watts through 683; a sun converts lux the same way, because the quantity it is stated in is different but the constant is not.\n\n0.526 DEGREES IS THE REAL SUN'S ANGULAR DIAMETER, and it is the reason a sunbeam edge is nearly sharp but not quite. Widen it and the whole scene goes hazy — it is the fastest soft-shadow control in the file and the easiest one to get wrong.\n\nTHE LOOK ENUM IS CARRIED AS TEXT, VERBATIM. Blender's is `\"AgX - Base Contrast\"`; there is no `\"AgX - Medium Contrast\"`, and a near-miss fails silently at the end of a three-minute render. A select would have to be kept in step with a Blender release; a string that is copied through cannot drift.\n\nLIGHT GROUPS ARE THE POINT OF ALL OF THIS. Tag every fitting into a group and the render comes back with one pass per group, so the whole panel of sliders becomes post-render knobs and re-tuning the key against the cove costs nothing instead of costing another render.\n\nWHAT THIS CANNOT DO. It does not render, and it does not check that the engine can do what is asked — EEVEE Next ignores most of the bounce settings, and saying so is the documentation's job, not a metric's.",
